# Why 45° goes farthest
subtitle: "Split a throw into speed across and time aloft to see why 45 degrees wins, and why 30° and 60° land on the very same spot."

## Why 45° goes farthest
domain: {"x":[0,49.8462],"y":[-6.4615,29.2308]}
vars: {"a":70}
use: projectile_motion
with: {"speed":10,"angle_deg":45,"show_components":true}

    curve traj dashed #8a5fa8 expr "x*tan(a*pi/180) - x^2/(80*cos(a*pi/180)^2)" x_from 0 x_to 40 bind.x_to 40*sin(2*a*pi/180) hidden true
    label l_traj "{a:0}°" above #8a5fa8 attach_to traj hidden true
    dot land #8a5fa8 at.x 40 at.y 0 bind {"at.x":"40*sin(2*a*pi/180)"} hidden true
    math range tex "R = \\frac{v^2\\,{\\sin 2\\theta}}{g}" size 34 colors {"\\theta":"#8a5fa8"} x 760 y 590 hidden true
    text t_launch "20 m/s at 45°" above v0 gap 30 font_size 28 hidden true
    text t_up "14 up" left vy_0 gap 10 font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_across "14 across" below vx_0 gap 10 font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_apex "top: 1.4 s" above v_apex gap 12 font_size 28 hidden true
    text t_land "2.8 s · 40 m" above v_land gap 40 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Why 45° goes farthest"

Why does a ball launched at 45 degrees go farthest? At a fixed speed, the angle alone decides. Here, 20 metres a second, at 45.
    draw ground path t_launch

Split the launch velocity in two: about fourteen metres a second across, and fourteen up.
    draw v0 vx_0 vy_0 t_up t_across

Gravity takes 10 metres a second off the upward part every second. At the top, 1.4 seconds in, only the 14 across is left.
    draw v_apex apex_label t_apex

It lands 2.8 seconds after launch, as fast as it left, the upward part now pointing down.
    draw v_land vx_1 vy_1 t_land

The range is the speed across times the time in the air: fourteen times 2.8, about forty metres.
    highlight path

The angle trades one against the other. Keep the speed, and tilt the launch.
    erase v0 vx_0 vy_0 v_apex apex_label v_land vx_1 vy_1 t_launch t_up t_across t_apex

At seventy degrees it climbs high and stays up longer, but crawls across: it lands at about twenty-six metres.
    draw traj l_traj land

    curve ghost70 dashed #8a5fa8 expr "x*tan(70*pi/180) - x^2/(80*cos(70*pi/180)^2)" x_from 0 x_to 25.712 style.opacity 0.5
    text l_ghost70 "70°" above ghost70 gap 10 #8a5fa8 font_size 26

Now lower it. At 45 the range peaks, out at forty metres,
    animate a 45 duration 2.5

then shrinks again: at 20 degrees it races across but drops within 1.4 seconds.
    animate a 20 duration 3

    erase t_land

Twenty-six metres again, the same spot. Any two angles that add up to ninety land together.
    text t_26 "26 m" below land gap 24 #8a5fa8 font_size 28

In a formula, the range goes with sine of twice the angle, which peaks at ninety, when the angle is forty-five.
    draw range

Galileo proved both results in 1638: forty-five goes farthest, and angles equally above and below it land together.
    highlight range part \sin2\theta

Real air drags hardest on long, high flights, so outside the textbook the best angle is somewhat lower.
    highlight path

    quiz "At this speed, a ball launched at 30° lands 35 metres away. Which other angle lands at the same spot?"
        right: Sixty degrees: thirty and sixty add up to ninety, so what one angle gains in time aloft the other gains in speed across.
        + 60°
        * 45°
        * 15°

## Thumbnail
role: thumbnail
page: {"valign": "none"}
    path arc smooth thick #c7702e points [[100,270],[280,560],[500,660],[720,560],[900,270]]
    icon ball size 120 set twemoji x 500 y 660 icon_look picture of baseball
    text a "45°" x 260 y 380 font_size 110 #2f6b8f
    text b "30° = 60°?" x 730 y 380 font_size 70 #b5482e
    thumb w1 "Two angles, one landing spot" kind band

